+A retry with the same idempotency key and an identical payload returns the original acceptance result — the same revision ID — and creates no new revision. Reuse of the key with a different payload is rejected and leaves the original submission unchanged. The acceptance result reports the assigned revision ID, the visit ID, the server receipt time, and whether the visit is now resolved or unresolved.
+
+## Retrieval
+
+Current-state retrieval reports the visit's state:
+
+- When resolved, it returns the current revision ID, that revision's scientific field values, its author, and its server receipt time.
+- When unresolved, it returns no designated revision and no field values. It returns the competing revision IDs with their author IDs and server receipt times, so a coordinator can order and compare them.
+
+Submission-history retrieval returns every accepted revision for the visit with its base linkage, author, receipt time, and payload, in server receipt order. History is retained after a visit is resolved and after later revisions arrive.
+
+## Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves unresolved review work by designating one complete revision as the visit's current revision. The designated revision is either a competing revision as submitted or a complete revision authored by the coordinator from one competing revision. Resolution never produces a field-level combination of revisions.
+
+A resolution records the coordinator's identity, the designated revision ID, the contributing competing revision IDs, and a resolution timestamp. The competing revisions remain stored and retrievable; the resolution sets them aside as review outcome rather than deleting them.
+
+Because two coordinators may work at once, a resolution names the set of competing revision IDs it is resolving. If the visit's unresolved set has changed since the coordinator fetched it, the resolution is rejected as stale and the visit stays unresolved.
+
+After resolution, a submission whose base equals the new current revision extends it normally. A submission based on any other revision — including one based on a set-aside competing revision — reopens the visit as unresolved, keeping the earlier resolution in history.
+
+## Downstream Contracts
+
+Tablet-sync sizes its local storage and retry state machine around the submission envelope and the acceptance result, retains the base revision ID of each draft, and displays whether a submitted visit is resolved or awaiting review.
+
+Review-export sizes its coordinator interface around current-state retrieval and submission-history retrieval, and builds its comparison view from the competing revisions of an unresolved visit. Only resolved visits can enter the exported CSV; the export set includes a visit under its current revision once it is resolved.
+
+Both nodes link back to this node rather than define concurrency semantics of their own.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions; see [decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md) | Field-aware three-way merge | Whole-visit revisions preserve a worker's submitted visit as a unit and avoid maintained merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits without noticing. The cost is a larger review queue, accepted in exchange for not risking silent scientific combination. |
+| Conflict detection | Compare a submission's base revision to the visit's current revision; any non-current base produces a competing revision | Field-level diff; last-writer-wins; arrival-order authority | Field-level diff requires the same merge rules this policy rejects. Last-writer-wins and arrival-order authority would silently discard a submitted edit. |
+| Resolution shape | Designate one complete revision as current | Field-level merge at review; automatic selection of a revision | A complete revision keeps the reviewed record explainable end to end. Automatic selection would choose between edits the server cannot rank scientifically. |
+| Unresolved retrieval | Return no field values, only competing revision summaries | Return the most recent revision; return the base revision | Any single value set would present one worker's edit as current before a coordinator has resolved the visit. |
+| Concurrent resolutions | Resolution names the competing set it resolves; a changed set is rejected as stale | Last-writer-wins; server-held lock | Two coordinators operate independently; rejecting a stale resolution surfaces the conflict instead of silently overwriting one coordinator's work. |
+| Idempotency | Key plus identical-payload comparison | Key alone; no idempotency | Connection failures are expected offline; key alone would let a corrected retry masquerade as the original submission. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions (2026-11-11).
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether two competing revisions with identical field values should resolve automatically instead of entering review.
+2. Whether a coordinator may designate a non-tip revision, or resolution is restricted to the visit's current tips.
+3. Visit withdrawal or deletion semantics; this node currently retains every accepted revision for the life of the visit.
+4. Retention duration for revisions set aside by a resolution.
